{"t":19.134145548,"dir":"in","msg":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"figaro.aria","params":{"parts":[{"turn":1,"inquiry":"Use the write tool exactly once to write /var/tmp/tapes/opera2.md — 25 numbered lines about Il barbiere di Siviglia. Then reply with one short sentence.","inquiry_segments":[{"sender":"aria e83ae209","text":"Use the write tool exactly once to write /var/tmp/tapes/opera2.md — 25 numbered lines about Il barbiere di Siviglia. Then reply with one short sentence."}],"sealed":false,"live":{"from":0,"v":1,"nodes":[{"id":0,"set":{"input":"{\"path\": \"/var/tmp/tapes/opera2.md\", \"conte"}}]},"from":0}],"more":{},"metrics":{"context_tokens":39,"context_limit":1000000,"context_exact":false,"tokens_in":0,"tokens_out":0,"cache_read_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"mantra":"Use the write tool exactly once to write /var/tmp/tapes/oper…"}}}}
{"t":19.192393679,"dir":"in","msg":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"figaro.aria","params":{"parts":[{"turn":1,"inquiry":"Use the write tool exactly once to write /var/tmp/tapes/opera2.md — 25 numbered lines about Il barbiere di Siviglia. Then reply with one short sentence.","inquiry_segments":[{"sender":"aria e83ae209","text":"Use the write tool exactly once to write /var/tmp/tapes/opera2.md — 25 numbered lines about Il barbiere di Siviglia. Then reply with one short sentence."}],"sealed":false,"live":{"from":0,"v":2,"nodes":[{"id":0,"set":{"args":{"content":"# Il barbiere di Siviglia\n\n1. An opera buffa in two acts by Gioachino Rossini.\n2. Libretto by Cesare Sterbini, after Beaumarchais's 1775 comedy.\n3. Premiered at the Teatro Argentina, Rome, on 20 February 1816.\n4. Rossini composed it in roughly three weeks, at age twenty-three.\n5. The premiere was a famous fiasco — hecklers, a stumble, a stray cat onstage.\n6. By the second night the audience had reversed itself into acclaim.\n7. It was first billed as *Almaviva, ossia L'inutile precauzione*, out of deference to Paisiello.\n8. Paisiello's own 1782 *Barbiere* had loyal partisans in Rome.\n9. The overture was recycled from *Aureliano in Palmira* and *Elisabetta*.\n10. Count Almaviva, tenor, woos Rosina in disguise as the student Lindoro.\n11. Rosina, a mezzo-soprano (often sung by sopranos), is the ward of Doctor Bartolo.\n12. Bartolo, a buffo bass, intends to marry her himself and keep her dowry.\n13. Don Basilio, music master and bass, sells slander as a professional service.\n14. Figaro the barber is the factotum who makes every scheme run.\n15. \"Largo al factotum\" is his baritone calling card — *Figaro qua, Figaro là*.\n16. \"Una voce poco fa\" is Rosina's declaration of a sweet but stubborn will.\n17. \"La calunnia è un venticello\" traces gossip from a breeze to a cannon shot.\n18. Act I ends in a delirious ensemble of confusion and arrested soldiers.\n19. Almaviva's second disguise is Don Alonso, a substitute singing teacher.\n20. The \"music lesson\" scene has long invited interpolated showpieces.\n21. A thunderstorm interlude opens the second half of Act II.\n22. The ladder is removed; the lovers are nearly trapped by their own plan.\n23. Basilio is bribed with a ring and the sight of a pistol.\n24. The marriage contract is signed before Bartolo can object, and he relents.\n25. Two centuries on, it remains among the most performed comic operas in the world.\n","path":"/var/tmp/tapes/opera2.md"},"summary":"/var/tmp/tapes/opera2.md"},"unset":["input"]}]},"from":0}],"more":{},"metrics":{"context_tokens":39,"context_limit":1000000,"context_exact":false,"tokens_in":0,"tokens_out":0,"cache_read_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"mantra":"Use the write tool exactly once to write /var/tmp/tapes/oper…"}}}}
